[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) said one issue, one fix queued. Its own table says otherwise: the
reports merged into it have two causes, and a few of them are not a flip at all. This page splits
#4471 into the problems engineering had to fix, one ticket each, and says where each one ended up.

<Callout>
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October. <code>parser</code> is fixed ("fix(dates): parse dd/mm for
eu locales"). <code>hint</code> won't be fixed: the booking-v3 form is retired.
</Callout>

## The problems

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

| problem | ticket | state |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser` | [#1](https://github.com/a12233/kb-4471/issues/1) | fixed |
| `hint` | [#2](https://github.com/a12233/kb-4471/issues/2) | won't fix |
| one-day move | [#3](https://github.com/a12233/kb-4471/issues/3) | open |

Each ticket carries the count from the table above. The one-day moves are the reports in the note
under the table: they are counted under `parser` and tracked again in #3.

### `parser`: the shared parser read dates month first

A day-first tenant writes `07/04` for 7 April, and the parser read 4 July. These reports came in by
app, by email and through booking-v2's form. Fixed: the parser now reads dd/mm for EU locales.

### `hint`: booking-v3's form told people to type the month first

booking-v3 printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then read the date the tenant's way, day
first. People typed what the hint said, and day and month swapped. Only reports sent through the
booking-v3 form, at booking-v3 tenants, have this cause. The table marks it "no ticket of its own";
it has one now, #2. Won't fix: the form is retired, so no new reports can come in this way.

### One-day move: dates land a day early or late

Not a flip. "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May." "Client in Lisbon got
Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." The date moves by one day, which points at time zones, not
at day and month order. The desk read these in the report bodies (`desk-notes.json`). By tenant
they are counted under `parser`. The parser fix does not explain them, so #3 stays open until
someone checks them against it.

<Callout kind="warn">
Closing #4471 does not close the one-day moves. Their ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## How the reports are counted

- One report per id. The export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
- The cause comes from the tenant's locale and booking form, not from intake's tag. Intake guesses
  from the reporter's browser, and the guess is often wrong: compare "tagged as" in the table.
- The counts are the desk's own view, as exported.

## Lessons

- A page can say one thing while its own table says another. Read the table.
- Merging on subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) put three problems in
  one ticket. A subject names the symptom, and all three share it: the wrong day.

